1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,096
You hit eight hours, alarm goes off, and you're already reaching for coffee before your feet touch the floor — sound familiar?

2
00:00:07,416 --> 00:00:12,883
Bloodwork comes back clean, no diagnosis in sight, and the exhaustion just doesn't add up.

3
00:00:13,203 --> 00:00:18,401
Three ordinary daily habits can quietly drain your energy no matter how much you sleep.

4
00:00:18,721 --> 00:00:26,837
Run short on fluids, short on key minerals, or short on real melatonin at night, and tomorrow feels heavy regardless of the hours logged.

5
00:00:27,157 --> 00:00:28,686
First, running low on water.

6
00:00:29,006 --> 00:00:39,302
Studies on mild dehydration — as little as one and a half percent of body weight lost in fluid — found it raised fatigue and dulled focus before anyone even felt thirsty.

7
00:00:39,622 --> 00:00:43,361
Coffee and soda don't refill that the way plain water does.

8
00:00:43,681 --> 00:00:45,791
Second, falling short on magnesium.

9
00:00:46,111 --> 00:00:57,186
NIH data shows close to half of American adults take in less magnesium than they need, and that's the mineral cells use to turn food into energy — running low leaves that process short-changed all day.

10
00:00:57,506 --> 00:01:00,027
Third, scrolling on a bright phone before bed.

11
00:01:00,347 --> 00:01:09,709
One study found two hours of screen time before sleep cut melatonin by nearly half, pushing you out of the deep, restorative sleep stages even after eight hours in bed.

12
00:01:10,029 --> 00:01:22,351
Carry a water bottle through the day, add magnesium-rich spinach, almonds, or pumpkin seeds to a meal, and put the phone down an hour before lights out — small habits that shape how heavy tomorrow feels.
